{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. We have some fun \nnew merch at the merch store,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3560,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ll let you know more at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The primary characteristic that defines \nblack holes is in the name. Black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are black. The gravitational pull at \nthe event horizon is so powerful that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not even light can escape. In this case, \nblack means absence of light. But we also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20840,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think of black as indicating absence of \ncolour. But it turns out there is a way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26640,
    "dDurationMs": 12680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to make a coloured black hole—as long as by \ncolour you mean quantum chromodynamic charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39320,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two main types of black holes that we \ndefinitely know exist. There are the stellar black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44400,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes that might be 10-50 times the mass of the \nSun. These are the ones left behind when the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive stars end their lives in supernova \nexplosions— and these things are physically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny on astronomical scales, with event horizons \nonly tens of kilometers across. Then we have the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60040,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supermassive black holes in the centers \nof galaxies, weighing in at millions to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63960,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions of times the Sun’s mass with radii on \nthe scale of solar systems. Mass seems to be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70400,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most important property distinguishing one black \nhole from another because it determines the size  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74680,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of their event horizon. The rotation of a black \nhole—its spin—is the next most important because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it squishes that otherwise spherical event horizon \nand drags the fabric of space around a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88720,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But according to the no-hair theorem \nof Jacob Bekenstein, black holes can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93480,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have only three properties and only three—mass, \nspin, and charge. Now we covered mass and spin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so what about charge? Normally when we \nthink about black hole charge we think  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about electric charge—the stuff that electrons \nand protons have and that shocks you when you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "drag your feet on the carpet then touch \na doorknob. Black holes have a hard time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116920,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holding onto electric charge because they quickly \nattract the opposite charge from matter in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding universe and neutralize. They are in \ncontact with the cosmic doorknob, if you like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128880,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the charge in the no-hair theorem \ndoesn’t just apply to the positive and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132920,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative electric charge that feels the \nelectromagnetic force. It refers to any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136800,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the charges corresponding to one \nof the fundamental quantum forces. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141320,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could there be black holes that hold \nonto these weirder types of charge?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most likely candidate is for a black \nhole to be charged with the so-called colour  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149960,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge of the strong force. We \nhave episodes on colour charge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154080,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong force, and the theory describing \nit—quantum chromodynamics. But in short,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159240,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong force holds quarks \ntogether in protons and neutrons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162240,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and holds those together in atomic nuclei. \nThere is one type of charge associated with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167560,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electromagnetic interaction - electric charge, \nwhich can be positive or negative. But there are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three types of charge for the strong force. \nThey’re named after colours: red, green, blue,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with their “negatives” being anti-red, anti-green \nand anti-blue. They aren’t actually these colours,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the naming is not an entirely a poetic choice. \nThere’s a deeper correspondence to how visible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colours and strong-force colours are related \nto each other, which we explain in this video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194560,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so the no-hair theorem tells us that black \nholes can exhibit any of the charges of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199200,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental forces, so we should be able \nto have a red or an anti-blue black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205640,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a quantum chromodynamic perspective. \nBut could these really exist out there  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe? And if so, might they have \nsome real influence on the universe that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213640,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could detect? According to a study published \nthis May by PhD student Elba Alonso Monsalve and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220880,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "professor David I. Kaiser at MIT. , the answer \nis yes and yes. Or at least maybe and maybe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason you probably haven’t heard about \ncolour-charged black holes before is because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at first glance they shouldn’t exist. \nI mentioned that electrically charged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes aren’t really a thing because \nthey neutralize themselves. Colour-charged  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241560,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes should be even harder to make. That’s \nbecause of something called colour confinement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In almost all environments in the modern universe \nit's impossible to observe an isolated color  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254160,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge, like a single quark. All color charges \nneed to coalesce together to form color-neutral  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects, particles called hadrons. So you’ll \nnever see a single green quark—you’ll see a green,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a red and a blue quark bound together into a \nproton or neutron. Or maybe a red and anti-red  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quark in a meson. The colour combination \nof these quark composites, or hadrons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will cancel out to zero colour charge. As \nreal black holes grow from consuming these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bound particles, they never have a \nchance to build up colour charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291520,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t mean it’s impossible to \nseparate colour charge. Colour confinement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "works at temperatures below 10 trillion Kelvin. \nBut above that temperature, individual quarks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301840,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gluons are no longer bound and can fly around \nfreely in a quark-gluon plasma. In such a plasma  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s possible to have little patches of more \ngreen or anti-red or whatever. If we could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314760,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create a black hole within such a patch we could \npresumably create a colour-charged black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, as it happens, there was a time when the \nuniverse was filled with a quark-gluon plasma,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and by chance we expect that in that time \nmany, many black holes may have formed. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333600,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may stand to reason that colourful black holes \nwere once the most natural thing in the world."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339400,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, right after the big bang, the universe \nconsisted of an extremely hot and dense soup of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344640,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter and radiation. It was also very slightly \nclumpy—there was a little more matter here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little less there—fluctuations that \npulled on surrounding material due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354120,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their slightly higher gravity, causing them to \neventually grow into the bumps of the cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359760,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "background radiation, and eventually into \ngalaxies. But here and there the size of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the density fluctuations were large enough to \ngrow even more quickly. Quickly enough to form  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370160,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes. These first black holes are called \nprimordial black holes. We’ve never found one,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they could be everywhere. Of \ncourse we did episodes on PBHs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because these things didn’t form from the cores \nof dead stars, PBHs could potentially have any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387000,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass. From as small as a millionth of a gram \nto as big as hundreds of thousands times the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sun's mass. If they formed at the massive \nend they may have been the first seeds of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supermassive black holes and may help explaining \nhow those things got so big so fast. If at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lighter end they may be a good candidate for \ndark matter. The mass these things formed at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409040,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depends on exactly when they formed in the \nfirst second-ish after the big bang. If early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414920,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then there should be lots of smaller PBHs. If \nlater then there should be fewer but larger ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420960,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so in the extreme conditions of that \nfirst second the universe may have made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countless black holes. If that black hole \ngeneration happened on the early side—in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431240,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first one-100,000th of a second—then we have \nthe conditions we need for colorful black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438840,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before 10^-5 seconds the temperature of the \nuniverse everywhere was higher than 10^13 Kelvin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is the temperature at which bound \nstates of quarks can’t exist. So back then the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire universe was filled with free quarks and \ngluons—what we call a quark-gluon plasma. Although  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456880,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the net colour charge of this plasma was zero, \nif you zoom in close enough on little patches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you would find regions that are randomly a bit \nmore of one colour than the others. And if such  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions were dense enough to collapse into a PBH, \nthat would give us our colour-charged black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The scientists behind this new study wanted to \nfigure out whether these colour-charged PBHs might  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist, and in what quantities. To determine \nthat they first needed to know when these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things formed—remember, it’s the formation \ntime that determines the size of the PBHs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493640,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the size of the PBHs determines \nwhether they are small enough to capture  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of these tiny colour-charged \nregions of the quark-gluon plasma."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These researchers use another assumption to choose \nthat formation time. I mentioned that some people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509000,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "think PBHs could explain dark matter. But that \nonly works for a narrow range of primordial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole masses, because our observations have \nruled out there being lots of black holes outside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this range. That range is roughly between \nthat of a medium asteroid and a small moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525720,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or 10^14-10^19 kg. If PBHs are dark matter \nthen they have to be in this mass range,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they must have formed between 10^-21 \nand 10^-16 seconds after the big bang,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541960,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this is the formation \nrange the researchers consider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545240,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe at that age is well \nwithin the quark-gluon plasma period,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549840,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so PBHs formed then could potentially have \ncolour charge if they were small enough to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554680,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "land in one of these random fluctuations of \ncolour. Unfortunately, most of the PBHs that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would have formed at this time, if they formed \nat this time, would be too big to capture any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "considerable amount of net color charge. \nSuch PBHs would have formed color neutral."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s still hope for coloured black \nholes. One of the main scenarios for PBH  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576800,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation does not form all of the black holes \nwith the same mass. Rather there’s a peak mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s the mass that would contribute the \nmost to dark matter. But there should also be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587000,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a long tail of smaller and smaller black hole \nmasses that form alongside these bigger ones.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of these would actually be small enough \nto capture significant colour charge. In the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598240,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scenarios explored by the researchers an enormous \nnumber of tiny, colorful PBHs should have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed alongside the larger, neutral PBHs \nthat would go on to become dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610840,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to capture significant colour \ncharge, a PBH would need to have a mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller than around 20 tons, which would make \nits event horizon ten thousand times smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619840,
    "dDurationMs": 8920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a proton. But these things could hold \na lot of colour change—more than any other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628760,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colored object previously studied. In fact, a \nsubset of these color charged black holes would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form with near maximum possible amount of color \ncharge that black holes can theoretically have:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these are called extremal, or near-extremal \nblack holes, in which the internal colour charge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647600,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "counteracts the internal gravitational field to \nthe point of almost nullifying the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, we do have one more problem before we \ncan start building our coloured black hole  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detectors. All black holes leak away their mass \nover time due to Hawking radiation. The minimum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass black hole that could still be around today \nis around 100 billion kg. The smaller the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole, the faster they leak. Any black hole small \nenough to have significant colour charge would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678480,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be long gone. In fact, a 20 ton black hole \nevaporates in one-ten-thousandth of a second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685680,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That doesn’t sound like very long, but compared to \nthe rapidly changing state of the early universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691960,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this was an aeon. It’s something like 100 \ntrillion times longer than the age of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697280,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe at which those PBHs formed. In \nfact, that 10^-4 second evaporation time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704520,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would allow those coloured PBHs to exist well \nbeyond the end of the quark-gluon plasma epoch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712240,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and enter into the era of color confinement \nwhen hadrons start to form. That potentially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717720,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "makes them the only colour-charged \nobjects in the universe at that time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723760,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But do they keep that colour charge? I mentioned \nthat electrically charged black holes quickly lose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729400,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that charge. But for those early colour-charged \nPBHs we have reason to hope that they maintain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their charge. Remember that these things are \ntiny—less than one ten thousandth the size of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741680,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a proton. That means they absorb material \nvery slowly. Also, once the colored black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749320,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole becomes surrounded by color neutral hadrons \nafter the quark-gluon epoch is over, a cloud of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757160,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual colored particles could form around it \nfrom the quantum vacuum and provide screening,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and make it behave like an almost color \nneutral hadron. For one thing it would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769200,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stop attracting opposite charges, allowing it \nto maintain its own internal charge for longer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, this screening process could have left \nan imprint in the form of gravitational waves:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781840,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although these would probably be too faint \nand high-frequency to detect with current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detectors. Some of these black holes could \nhave also lasted much longer into the era  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790360,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of big bang nucleosynthesis, the next \nchapter in the history of the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the first sight nuclei began \nforming from protons and neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800000,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The surviving colored black holes would be the \nnear-extremal ones, the ones with most color,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which happen to have the lowest Hawking \ntemperature and thus evaporate slower.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These might have changed the distribution \nof hadrons and disturbed the formation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclei in such a way that we may be able \nto find a discrepancy in the ratio of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "numbers of light elements generated in \nthat period. This would be the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hint and evidence of this kind that points \nto any of this having actually happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831720,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is one possible way that colour-charged \nblack holes might exist in the modern universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some physicists believe that black holes don’t \never fully evaporate, but instead stop when their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size reaches the smallest definable length—the \nPlanck length. These are called Planck relics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Primordial black holes that started out smaller \nthan a trillion kg would now be Planck relics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856920,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if there are enough of them they \ncould also make up dark matter. And,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861200,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another in a chain of if’s—if everything \nI just described is the case, then these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867000,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still-existent Planck relics could potentially \nhave colour charge. That would be great,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because there’s almost no way to detect \nneutral Planck relics. Colour charge may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give them an interaction with atomic nuclei, \nwhich means we could build a detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883040,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As cool as all of this sounds, are these \ncolored black holes actually thing? We  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t have direct observational access to \nthat chaotic first fraction of a second.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895120,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the theoretical basis for their \nformation is solid, and if this is right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it could lead to real, observable effects \nthat help us refine our understanding of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the early universe. They may even help solve \nthe problem of dark matter. We just need the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910480,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe to have started out with a sprinkling of \nmulticoloured holes in its fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’d like to support this show, please \nsubscribe, and do click that notification  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923080,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bell. And maybe buy some merch. We just \nreleased our very first patch—its cool—as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929400,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "well as a new enamel pin. Link below. And another \nincredibly effective way to support us is through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935680,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patreon, where from a couple of bucks a \nmonth will get you access to our discord,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940280,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monthly live chats with me, and much more. Today \nI’d like to extend a special thank you to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947640,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First Principles Foundation for supporting \nus at the Big Bang level. We put together  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953200,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a nice box of assorted colour-charge primordial \nblack holes to thank First Principles for their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong and forceful support. Unfortunately we \nincluded all colours and anti-colours and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancelled out, then evaporated in a ten-thousandth \nof a second. But we’re pretty sure they would have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970160,
    "dDurationMs": 18760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been delicious. Anyway, we hope you’ll accept \nour heartfelt, if colour-neutral thanks instead."
    } ]
  } ]
}
